====Verb====
{{en-verb|past2=burnt|past2_qual=mostly Commonwealth|past3=brent|past3_qual=obsolete}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to be consumed by [[fire]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''burned''' his manuscript in the fireplace.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=29|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/asia/21580154-forest-fires-bring-record-levels-air-pollution-and-end-not-sight-unspontaneous Unspontaneous combustion]
|passage=Since the mid-1980s, when Indonesia first began to clear its bountiful forests on an industrial scale in favour of lucrative palm-oil plantations, “haze” has become an almost annual occurrence in South-East Asia. The cheapest way to clear logged woodland is to '''burn''' it, producing an acrid cloud of foul white smoke that, carried by the wind, can cover hundreds, or even thousands, of square miles.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be consumed by [[fire]], or in [[flame]]s.
#: {{ux|en|He watched the house '''burn'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21581981-what-pollution-some-opportunity-others-welcome-plastisphere Welcome to the plastisphere]
|passage=Plastics are energy-rich substances, which is why many of them '''burn''' so readily. Any organism that could unlock and use that energy would do well in the Anthropocene. Terrestrial bacteria and fungi which can manage this trick are already familiar to experts in the field.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To overheat so as to make unusable.
#: {{ux|en|He '''burned''' the toast. The blacksmith '''burned''' the steel.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[overheat]]ed to the point of being unusable.
#: {{ux|en|The grill was too hot and the steak '''burned'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make or produce by the application of fire or burning heat.
#: {{ux|en|to '''burn''' a hole;&emsp; to '''burn''' letters into a block}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=78|passage=I posted myself near a place where they had been '''burning''' charcoal, and very soon the hare came running past, close to where I was standing.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[injure]] (a person or animal) with [[heat]] or [[chemicals]] that produce similar [[damage]].
#: {{ux|en|She '''burned''' the child with an iron, and was jailed for ten years.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|surgery}} To [[cauterize]].
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[sunburn]].
#: {{ux|en|She forgot to put on sunscreen and '''burned'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To consume, damage, or change the condition of, as if by action of fire or heat; to affect as fire or heat does.
#: {{ux|en|to '''burn''' the mouth with pepper}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|act=V|scene=iii|passage=This tyrant fever '''burns''' me up.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Spanish Fryar
|passage=This dry sorrow '''burns''' up all my tears.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1965|title=Amplified Bible|section=James 4:2
|passage=You are jealous and covet [what others have] and your desires go unfulfilled; [so] you become murderers. [To hate is to murder as far as your hearts are concerned.] You '''burn''' with envy and anger and are not able to obtain [the gratification, the contentment, and the happiness that you seek], so you fight and war. You do not have, because you do not ask.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[hot]], e.g. due to [[embarrassment]].
#: {{ux|en|The child's forehead was '''burning''' with fever.&emsp; Her cheeks '''burned''' with shame.}}
#* {{ante|1542}}, {{w|Thomas Wyatt (poet)|Thomas Wyatt}}, “[[wikibooks:The Devonshire Manuscript/What menythe thys when I lye alone|What menythe thys]]” in the [[wikibooks:The Devonshire Manuscript|Devonshire Manuscript]], folio 12 verso:
#*: In ſlumbers oft for fere I quake<br>For hete & cold I '''burne''' & ſhake<br>For lake of ſlepe my hede dothe ake<br>What menys thys
# {{lb|en|chemistry|transitive}} To cause to combine with oxygen or other active agent, with evolution of heat; to consume; to oxidize.
#: {{ux|en|A human being '''burns''' a certain amount of carbon at each respiration.&emsp; {{nowrap|to '''burn''' iron in oxygen}}}}
# {{lb|en|chemistry|dated}} To combine energetically, with evolution of heat.
#: {{ux|en|Copper '''burns''' in chlorine.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To write data to a permanent [[storage]] [[medium]] like a [[compact]] [[disc]] or a [[ROM]] chip.
#: {{ux|en|We’ll '''burn''' this program onto an EEPROM one hour before the demo begins.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2015-10-22|author=Laura Snapes|title=How Arctic Monkeys’ debut single changed the music industry and ‘killed the NME’|journal=w:The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2015/oct/22/arctic-monkeys-debut-single-i-bet-you-look-good-dancefloor|passage=At first they didn’t do anything by design, beyond writing songs and gigging around Sheffield, distributing homemade demo CDs at shows. Their canny friends '''burned''' copies to leave on buses, but more importantly, uploaded them to filesharing sites and set up a MySpace page.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing|by extension}} To [[render]] subtitles into a video's content while [[transcoding]] it, making the subtitles part of the image.
#: {{ux|en|My old DVD player could play DivX files but didn't recognize the subtitle file, so I had to '''burn''' them in.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[betray]].
#: {{ux|en|The informant '''burned''' him.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[insult]] or [[defeat]].
#: {{ux|en|I just '''burned''' you again.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[waste]] (time); to waste money or other resources.
#: {{ux|en|We have an hour to '''burn'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The company has '''burned''' more than a million dollars a month this year.}}
# In certain games, to approach near to a concealed object which is sought.
#: {{ux|en|You're cold... warm... hot... you're '''burning'''!}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1860|author=w:Henry David Thoreau|title=w:The Last Days of John Brown|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/70123/pg70123-images.html#Page_278
|passage=Not being accustomed to make fine distinctions, or to appreciate magnanimity, they read his letters and speeches as if they read them not. They were not aware when they approached a heroic statement,—they did not know when they '''burned'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|curling}} To accidentally touch a moving [[stone]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|cards}} In [[pontoon]], to swap a pair of cards for another pair, or to deal a [[dead]] card.
# {{lb|en|photography}} To increase the [[exposure]] for certain areas of a [[print]] in order to make them [[light]]er (compare {{m|en|dodge}}).
# {{lb|en|intransitive|physics|of an element}} To be converted to another [[element]] in a [[nuclear fusion]] reaction, especially in a [[star]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang|card games|gambling}} To [[discard]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[shoot]] someone with a [[firearm]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|espionage}} To [[compromise]] (an agent's [[cover story]]).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Thomas H. Cook|title=Night Secrets
|passage=He had already '''burned''' his cover with Mrs. Phillips, and it was not a mistake he intended to make again.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=Vanessa Kier|title=Vengeance: The SSU Book 1
|passage=Eventually they'd report back to Ryker, and he still didn't know if Ryker had personally '''burned''' his cover and sent assassins after him, or if the SSU had a mole. Until he knew for certain, he had to play this safe.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|espionage}} To [[blackmail]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=John le Carré|title=Smiley's People
|passage="''How'' does Leipzig '''burn''' him precisely?" Enderby insisted. "What's the pressure? Dirty pix—well, okay. Karla's a puritan, so's Kirov. But I mean, Christ, this isn't the fifties, is it? {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang|US}} To desire or [[ache for]] (something); to focus on attaining (something).

