===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰrewh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|burn}}, {{m|enm|bourne}}, from {{inh|en|ang|burne}}, {{m|ang|burna|t=spring, fountain}}, {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*brunnō}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*brunnô}}, {{m|gem-pro|*brunō}}. 

Cognate with {{cog|fy|boarne}}, {{cog|nl|bron}}, {{cog|de|Brunnen}}; also {{cog|sq|burim||spring, fountain}}, {{cog|grc|φρέαρ||well, reservoir}}, {{cog|xcl|աղբիւր||fount}}. {{doublet|en|bourn}}. More at {{l|en|brew}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|stream}} {{lb|en|Northern England|Scotland}} A [[stream#Noun|stream]].
#* {{RQ:Hopkins Poems|poem=Inversnaid|stanza=1|page=53|passage=This darksome '''burn''', horseback brown, / His rollrock highroad roaring down, / In coop and in comb the fleece of his foam / Flutes and low to the lake falls home.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1881|author=s:Robert Louis Stevenson|title=s:Virginibus Puerisque
|passage=He may pitch on some tuft of lilacs over a '''burn''', and smoke innumerable pipes to the tune of the water on the stones.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=October|title=Completion of Flood-Damage Repairs, East Coast Main Line|journal=Railway Magazine|pages=708-709|text=At this place, the clay in the 52 ft. embankment had been under water pressure for some weeks before the water level could be lowered, and the '''burn''' diverted through a temporary culvert.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=w:James Kelman|title=Kieron Smith, Boy|page=105|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2009
|passage=When it was too heavy rain the '''burn''' ran very high and wide and ye could never jump it.}}

